		<profileDesc>
			<abstract>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><p>Sync engines that replicate a primary database into a local replica must choose how far behind that replica is allowed to fall. We characterise the staleness a single-node deployment actually exhibits under <ref type="bibr">[2]</ref> ordinary read-heavy load.</p><p>We then show that a bound of one replication interval is sufficient for interactive applications, and report measurements from a deployment serving a personal document collection.</p></div>
			</abstract>
		</profileDesc>
